it's pretty rare to have a conversation  about education policy in which the word  technology isn't used at some point  occasionally in the administrative sense  as a way to make education more  efficient but mostly in the classroom  sense helping kids learn which gets us  to Salman Khan he's a former hedge fund  manager turned educator as the founder  and sole faculty member of the Khan  Academy it started as a tutoring service  for his cousin's on YouTube it's become  a library of thousands of instructional  videos on topics from basic arithmetic  to organic chemistry to the French  Revolution with financial backing from  among others the Gates Foundation Sal  Khan good to have you with us great to  be here how exactly did you wind up  putting videos on YouTube I was a little  dismissive of it at first I didn't think  YouTube was for serious mathematics I  thought it was more for for dogs on  skateboards or whatever else but but I  decided to give it a shot and people all  over the world started watching them and  you know some of them would say hey this  helped me out an exam but someone would  say hey this helped me not drop out of  high school or this helped me get into  the the school that I wanted to get into  so it became a pretty exciting thing to  work on what's the education problem  though that you think this technology  can solve it's a pretty straightforward  problem is is that everyone learns at  different paces and right now everyone's  forced to go at the same pace and it's a  more serious problem than just people  being bored or people being lost in one  classroom what happens when everyone  moves at the same pace is you and I are  in an exam or you know we're learning a  basic subject I I don't get five percent  of the subject you don't get twenty  percent of the subject the whole class  moves on to a more advanced subject I  have a five percent gap you have a  20-percent gap those gaps build up over  time and it pretty much guarantees that  most students by the time to get to  algebra calculus have so many of these  gaps that no matter how hard they try  they just can't understand what's going  on so what we're trying to address is  every student can go at their own pace  make sure that they master something  before moving on and when you do that  then those gaps shouldn't shouldn't  happen it's an accumulation of failure  right isn't that your friend yeah I  would say it's accumulation of gaps and  and and actually the failure thing is  another interesting you know right now  when you take an assessment to test it  in school you get a label you get an a B  or C or D you know the eight kids kind  of feel good about themselves they get  self-confidence  be students are a little bit neutral you  get a CD or F it's not viewed as hey you  didn't understand thirty percent why  don't you go and try to learn a little  bit better to do it as you're not so  smart don't try to become an engineer  physicist where do you fit in though  where does Khan Academy fit in and the  large application of technology to  education how do you slide in there for  a while whenever people talk about  technology and education they've just  been talking about putting computers or  iPads whatever in a room and and hoping  something good happens even when there  was something to do on a computer it was  always viewed as separate from the core  curriculum it was viewed separate of  what normally goes on in a math class  what we're saying is now we can actually  use technology we can use these videos  we can use this software to allow every  student to work at their own pace it's  not an either/or the proposition anymore  but there's always this debate if do you  do more of the core skills or do more if  the projects and the investigations  we're saying you can do both you can use  the technology to hand a lot of the core  skills free up a lot of the teachers  time to do more of the the human  interactions for the price of a login  right you either login through Facebook  or Google you get access to all these  things is there a thought in your mind  of somehow making people pay for this we  want at some point be sustainable as a  not-for-profit but we want all of this  material to be one hundred percent  accessible by everyone so on the web and  and our core tools will always be free  right right now nothing on our radar is  a non-free I guess it's a simple answer  so what's the next step for the Khan  Academy right now we have about two  million students a month who use our  site on a regular basis we want to start  leveraging them so that they can help  each other so if you're in a classroom  your teacher knows that you need help  and if you're not in the classroom or if  you need to supplement to a classroom  maybe one of the other community members  of the Khan Academy can tutor you or  help you out or get you past your hurdle  Salman Khan he's the founder of the Khan  Academy thanks a lot great thanks
 

